WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.268
Today is November 07, 2026.

3
00:00:07.268 --> 00:00:14.302
Overnight, Wall Street staged a tentative rebound after a brutal week driven by hawkish rates and midterm shocks.

4
00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:24.768
October nonfarm payrolls surged by 165,000 jobs, crushing the 65,000 consensus while prior months were revised up by 84,000.

5
00:00:24.768 --> 00:00:32.721
Unemployment unexpectedly dipped to 4.1 percent, and average hourly earnings rose 3.1 percent year over year.

6
00:00:32.721 --> 00:00:40.356
Two-year Treasury yields climbed to 4.54 percent, and ten-year yields reached 4.87 percent.

7
00:00:40.356 --> 00:00:46.238
Rate futures now price a 70 percent probability of a December Federal Reserve rate hike.

8
00:00:46.238 --> 00:00:53.089
San Francisco Fed President Mary Daly warned that sticky inflation may require even further rate increases.

9
00:00:53.089 --> 00:01:00.981
Meanwhile, the new Fed chair scrapped forward guidance, shifting policy to pure data dependence on inflation and employment.

10
00:01:00.981 --> 00:01:07.341
Combined with a Democratic midterm blue wave in Congress, both the Fed put and White House put vanished.

11
00:01:07.341 --> 00:01:15.625
On Friday, the S and P 500 edged up 0.31 percent, ending the week down 2.68 percent.

12
00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:23.983
The Nasdaq gained just 0.09 percent Friday, tumbling 4.9 percent this week for a fourth straight weekly loss.

13
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:32.427
The volatility index stays elevated above 25, after Thursday saw a record 1.5 billion dollars in put premium.

14
00:01:32.427 --> 00:01:42.182
Retail investors dumped 257 million dollars on Thursday, while volatility-control funds slashed equity exposure by 30 percentage points.

15
00:01:42.182 --> 00:01:51.679
Technical support for the S and P 500 sits at 7,240 to 7,250, matching the JPMorgan collar put strike.

16
00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:59.535
Until the Nasdaq reclaims its 200-day moving average, only long-term accumulation in broad index funds makes sense.

17
00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:06.471
Still, eight trillion dollars in sideline cash and active corporate buybacks should cushion near-term downside.

18
00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:13.665
This macro deleveraging collided directly with regulatory fears over artificial intelligence data center expansion.

19
00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:21.790
Incoming Democratic governors in Texas, Ohio, and Georgia proposed new taxes and pauses on power-hungry data centers.

20
00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:30.197
However, the Texas governor-elect confirmed that permitted projects covering 2027 and most of 2028 will finish construction.

21
00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:37.500
President Trump and White House economic director Hassett also pledged full federal support for superintelligence infrastructure.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:46.177
SemiAnalysis reports that 75 percent of 2027 data center projects across 20 key states are already under construction.

23
00:02:46.177 --> 00:02:54.535
JPMorgan notes 2027 power is secure, though 38 gigawatts of 2028 capacity face potential delays.

24
00:02:54.535 --> 00:03:01.777
Wells Fargo estimates that even halting half of Texas projects would trim national AI capex by just eight percent.

25
00:03:01.777 --> 00:03:09.510
Tech giants are already adapting, led by Google investing 17 billion dollars into clean-energy data centers in Wyoming.

26
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:18.738
The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index rebounded 4.2 percent Friday, with forward valuation compressed to just 12.7 times earnings.

27
00:03:18.738 --> 00:03:27.170
Cathie Wood bought the dip in Nvidia, A-M-D, and Google, while trimming Broadcom, Taiwan Semiconductor, and CoreWeave.

28
00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:35.258
Meanwhile, Anthropic begins its IPO roadshow next week after slashing its target valuation in half to one trillion dollars.

29
00:03:35.258 --> 00:03:42.255
That massive offering will fund upstream cloud and chip orders, but it will also drain secondary market liquidity.

30
00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:50.503
Consequently, institutional capital is rotating out of heavy capex builders into defensive hardware and high-margin AI software.

31
00:03:50.503 --> 00:03:58.983
Apple briefly topped 5.1 trillion dollars in market value on lower memory chip cost expectations and on-device AI safety.

32
00:03:58.983 --> 00:04:06.165
Yet rising cloud token costs for Siri upgrades still pose a medium-term headwind to Apple hardware margins.

33
00:04:06.296 --> 00:04:12.728
In individual equities, high-margin AI advertising software leads our actionable buy list today.

34
00:04:12.678 --> 00:04:19.884
AppLovin surged 30 percent Thursday and 10 percent Friday as third-quarter net income jumped 72 percent.

35
00:04:19.884 --> 00:04:28.671
Revenue climbed 55 percent year over year, and management expanded its share buyback authorization to 3.6 billion dollars.

36
00:04:28.671 --> 00:04:36.514
Unity also soared 40 percent this week, posting its first-ever quarterly net profit of 122 million dollars.

37
00:04:36.514 --> 00:04:45.313
Unity AI ad revenue surged 126 percent year over year, confirming rapid monetization across software applications.

38
00:04:45.313 --> 00:04:52.825
Traders should buy AppLovin on a confirmed breakout above its 200-day moving average, using that level as a stop-loss.

39
00:04:52.825 --> 00:05:01.122
Cloudflare offers a second actionable buy setup after third-quarter revenue grew 39 percent, its fastest pace in four years.

40
00:05:01.122 --> 00:05:08.377
Management raised full-year profit guidance for the third time as AI workloads drive enterprise cybersecurity demand.

41
00:05:08.377 --> 00:05:15.987
Buy Cloudflare on pullbacks holding the five-day moving average, targeting the 120-day line with a stop just below.

42
00:05:15.987 --> 00:05:23.352
Oracle also staged a sharp intraday reversal after rumors of Microsoft canceling cloud leases proved overblown.

43
00:05:23.352 --> 00:05:32.507
Microsoft only retired 30,000 aging A-one-hundred chips, leaving Oracle and its 500,000 modern AI chips fundamentally intact.

44
00:05:32.507 --> 00:05:41.392
In consumer tech, Airbnb jumped nearly 12 percent after third-quarter revenue rose 19 percent to 4.87 billion dollars.

45
00:05:41.392 --> 00:05:49.713
Management raised fourth-quarter revenue guidance to 3.28 billion dollars and announced a massive 12 billion dollar stock buyback.

46
00:05:49.713 --> 00:05:57.666
Traders can buy Airbnb on a breakout above its August swing high, while monitoring long-term AI agent disruption risks.

47
00:05:57.666 --> 00:06:05.178
On the sell side, investors should trim or avoid Tesla following a decisive technical breakdown and political blowback.

48
00:06:05.178 --> 00:06:12.825
Despite a four percent Friday bounce, Thursday's plunge broke below Tesla's primary uptrend line established in August.

49
00:06:12.825 --> 00:06:21.220
Elon Musk spent 474 million dollars backing Republicans, making his companies prime targets for the new Democratic Congress.

50
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:30.166
Senator Elizabeth Warren and House committees plan investigations into SpaceX federal contracts, Starlink, and Texas Terafab subsidies.

51
00:06:30.166 --> 00:06:39.124
While SpaceX rebounded eight percent to its 135 dollar IPO price, regulatory headwinds will cap Tesla's valuation multiple.

52
00:06:39.124 --> 00:06:46.441
Take profits on Tesla rallies below the broken August trendline, and avoid chasing rate-sensitive lenders like Affirm.

53
00:06:46.441 --> 00:06:50.448
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

54
00:06:50.448 --> 00:06:54.615
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
